# Day 3 — Git Branching Basics

## 1. What is Git Branching?
Git branching allows us to work on changes separately without directly affecting the main branch.

## 2. Check Branches
git branch
Shows the available local branches. The `*` symbol indicates the current branch.

## 3. Create a New Branch
git branch practice-branch
Creates a new branch named `practice-branch`.

## 4. Switch to a Branch
git switch practice-branch
Switches to the `practice-branch`.

## 5. Check Repository Status
git status
Shows the current branch and the status of files.

## 6. Add Changes
git add practice.txt
Stages the changes in `practice.txt`.

## 7. Commit Changes
git commit -m "Update practice branch"
Saves the staged changes as a commit on the current branch.

## 8. Switch Back to Main
git switch main
Switches back to the `main` branch.

## What I Practiced
- Checking the current branch
- Creating a new branch
- Switching between branches
- Editing a file on a separate branch
- Committing changes

